#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:638
msgid ""
"The tactic §0 proves logical tautologies.\n"
"\n"
"# Friends and relatives\n"
"\n"
"Sometimes the proof target must first be abstracted with §1 so that §2 recognizes the tautology."
msgstr ""
"The tactic §0 proves logical tautologies. \n"
"\n"
"## Friends and relations\n"
"\n"
" Sometimes the goal must first be abstracted with §1 so that §2 recognises the tautology."

#. §0: `fun_prop`
#. §1: `Continuous`
#. §2: `Measurable`
#. §3: `Differentiable`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:647
msgid ""
"The tactic §0 automatically discharges function-property goals such as §1,\n"
"§2, or §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h₁ : ∀ᶠ x in 𝓕, p₁ x`
#. §1: `∀ᶠ x in 𝓕, p x`
#. §2: `filter_upwards [h₁]'`
#. §3: `p₁ x → p x`
#. §4: `filter_upwards [h₁, ⋯, hₙ]`
#. §5: `hᵢ : ∀ᶠ x in 𝓕, pᵢ x`
#. §6: `p₁ x → ⋯ → pₙ x → p x`
#. §7: `filter_upwards [h₁, ⋯, hₙ] with x h₁' ⋯ hₙ'`
#. §8: `filter_upwards`
#. §9: `intro x h₁' ⋯ hₙ'`
#. §10: `∀ᶠ`
#. §11: `filter_upwards [h₁, h₂] with x h₁' h₂'`
#. §12: ```
#. h₁ : ∀ᶠ x in 𝓕, p₁ x
#. h₂ : ∀ᶠ x in 𝓕, p₂ x
#. ⊢ ∀ᶠ x in 𝓕, p x
#. ```
#. §13: ```
#. x : …
#. h₁ : p₁ x
#. h₂ : p₂ x
#. ⊢ p x
#. ```
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:653
msgid ""
"Suppose we have an \"eventual\" hypothesis §0\n"
"and an \"eventual\" goal §1.\n"
"The tactic §2 reduces the goal to the point-wise implication §3.\n"
"\n"
"# Variants\n"
"\n"
"- §4 works in the same way: given several \"eventual\" hypotheses\n"
"   §5, the goal is reduced to §6.\n"
"- §7 follows the tactic §8 with\n"
"  §9.\n"
"   So the total effect on the proof state is that §10 is removed.\n"
"   For example, §11 turns\n"
"§12\n"
"    into\n"
"§13"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h`
#. §1: `convert h`
#. §2: `h`
#. §3: `h`
#. §4: ```
#. h : HasDerivAt f a x
#. ⊢ HasDerivAt g a x
#. ```
#. §5: `convert h`
#. §6: ```
#. ⊢ g = f
#. ```
#. §7: `convert ← h`
#. §8: `f = g`
#. §9: `convert h using n`
#. §10: `h`
#. §11: `n`
#. §12: `convert`
#. §13: `convert! h`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:681
msgid ""
"If a hypothesis §0 is almost the goal, §1 closes the goal using §2\n"
"and creates new goals for the places where the goal and §3 differ.\n"
"\n"
"## Example\n"
"\n"
"§4\n"
"After §5, it remains to show\n"
"§6\n"
"\n"
"## Variants\n"
"\n"
"- §7 creates the new goals in the opposite direction (here §8).\n"
"- §9 only compares the goal and §10 up to depth §11.\n"
"  Use it when §12 breaks the goal into too many small (or even false) pieces.\n"
"- §13 additionally closes new goals that hold by definition."
msgstr ""

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L01_Tauto.lean:21
msgid "Intro Logo L01"
msgstr ""
"Right now, you're on Queen Logwynn's planet. She gets straight to the point:\n"
"\n"
"**Logwynn**: Honourable beings from a world unknown to us, welcome to the Leaniverse!  May I ask just one question?  Why is it that … \n"
"\n"
"And she scribbles something on a piece of paper: a few assumptions at the top, a conclusion at the bottom. In between, you are apparently supposed to write down a proof. You turn to Scribble with a helpless expression."

#. §0: `«{A}» «{B}» «{C}» : Prop`
#. §1: `«{A}»`
#. §2: `«{B}»`
#. §3: `«{C}»`
#. §4: `→`
#. §5: $⇒$
#. §6: `tauto`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L01_Tauto.lean:41
msgid ""
"Explain that §0 introduces statements §1, §2 und §3 as\n"
"propositions. The symbol §4 is equivalent to the implication symbol §5. Try §6."
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: That’s really simple. By §0 she means: §1, §2 and §3 are some statements (*propositions*). And by §4 she means §5, i.e. “implies”. You know the other symbols, right?\n"
"\n"
"**You**: Uhhm, yeah. But I still need to think about this for a moment.\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble** *(whispering)*: Just claim it’s a tautology.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: Seriously?\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Yeah. Just write §6.\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Come on …"

#. §0: `tauto`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L01_Tauto.lean:53
msgid "Conclusion Logo L01: Remind that §0 cannot be used"
msgstr "**Logwynn** *(somewhat taken aback)*:  Yes, strictly speaking, that is correct. But don't believe for one moment that this will get you very far on *this* planet! My subjects do not comprehend §0. You will have to try a little harder than that."

#. §0: $42 = 42$
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L02_Rfl.lean:20
msgid "Intro Logo L02: Why is §0?"
msgstr ""
"In the meantime, a long queue of subjects has formed, all eager to ask their questions. Logwynn beckons the first one forward. He clears his throat. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"**Subject**: Why is §0? \n"
"\n"
"You look at him in disbelief. He writes it down for you."

#. §0: `rfl`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L02_Rfl.lean:28
msgid "Remind: Equality is reflexive, so try §0"
msgstr "**Scribble**: But this is obvious. You just have to remind him that equality is *reflexive*. Try §0."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L02_Rfl.lean:37
msgid "Conclusion Logo L02"
msgstr "**Subject**: Ah, right. Yes, you're absolutely right. I keep forgetting. Rfl, rfl, rfl …"

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L03_Assumption.lean:15
msgid "Intro Logo L03"
msgstr "While the first subject is still murmuring rfl, rfl, rfl, the next one steps forward. He is shy and communicates only in writing."

#. §0: `«{n}» : ℕ`
#. §1: `«{n}»`
#. §2: `«{n}» ∈ ℕ`
#. §3: `«{h₁}»`
#. §4: `«{h₂}»`
#. §5: `«{h₃}»`
#. §6: `n < 10`
#. §7: `1 < n`
#. §8: `n ≠ 5`
#. §9: `1 < n`
#. §10: `assumption`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L03_Assumption.lean:40
msgid ""
"Explain that §0 means §1 is a natural number. The notation §2 is not used.\n"
"§3, §4, §5 are names for the respective assumptions §6, §7 und §8.\n"
"The proof goal is §9. Try §10"
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: §0 means §1 is a natural number.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: Then why doesn’t he write §2??\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Because everything here has weird types … I can explain it to you properly later. For now I just want to decode the question.\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: So, §3, §4, §5 are just names for different assumptions, namely for the assumptions §6, §7 and §8. What we’re supposed to prove is: §9.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: But that was just one of the assumptions.\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Yeah, true.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: ???\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: You just have to say so explicitly. Try §10."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L03_Assumption.lean:52
msgid "Conclusion Logo L03"
msgstr "**Subject**: Yes, that's right! If only you knew how much I've racked my brains over this question!"

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L04_Assumption.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L04"
msgstr "A third subject approaches you with the following problem."

#. §0: `«{A}» : Prop`
#. §1: `«{A}»`
#. §2: `«{hA}»`
#. §3: `«{A}»`
#. §4: `«{A}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L04_Assumption.lean:26
msgid ""
"Explain that §0 means that §1 is some statement.\n"
"§2 is the name of the assumption that §3 is true. Try to solve §4 using said assumption"
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: Here, §0 again means that §1 is some kind of statement. And §2 is a name for the assumption that §3 is true. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: And based on this assumption, we are now supposed to prove §4? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Yes. You can figure this one out yourself, can't you?"

#. §0: `assumption`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L04_Assumption.lean:33
msgid "Use §0 as goal is in assumptions"
msgstr "**Scribble**: It's just like before: the statement that needs to be proven is already one of the assumptions. So §0 will work again."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L04_Assumption.lean:39
msgid "Conclusion Logo L04"
msgstr "**Subject**: That was quick. Great! Thank you very much."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L05_True.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L05"
msgstr "The next subject in the queue is a rascal."

#. §0: `True`
#. §1: `decide`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L05_True.lean:26
msgid "Explain that §0 is a special statement that is always true. Try §1"
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: This §0 is a very special statement, namely the statement that is always and unconditionally true. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: So what exactly remains to be done here? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: §1dly little."

#. §0: `decide`
#. §1: `decide`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L05_True.lean:40
msgid "Conclusion Logo L05: §0 cannot be used every time. §1 works only in special cases"
msgstr ""
"**Rascal**: Just wanted to check you aren't fools … \n"
"\n"
"**You** *(to Scribble)*: Can't we just always use this §0? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: No, §1 only works in special situations where an algorithm is known that can decide whether the statement is true."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L06_Not.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L06"
msgstr "The rascal has brought his sister along."

#. §0: `¬`
#. §1: `(A : Prop)`
#. §2: `¬A`
#. §3: `False`
#. §4: `decide`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L06_Not.lean:30
msgid ""
"Explain that §0 is the negation sign. If §1 is true than §2 is false and\n"
"vica versa. §3 is the statement that is always false. Try §4"
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: This symbol §0 means negation. If §1 is true, then §2 is false, and vice versa. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: And §3 is probably the statement that is always false? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: True. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: So can't I use §4 again? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Perhaps."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L06_Not.lean:40
msgid "Conclusion Logo L06"
msgstr "The sister laughs, then rushes off to catch up with her brother."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L07_Contradiction.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L07"
msgstr "Next up are three troublemakers. The first one has the following problem:"

#. §0: `«{A}»`
#. §1: `«{h}»`
#. §2: `False`
#. §3: `False`
#. §4: `False`
#. §5: `«{A}»`
#. §6: `True`
#. §7: `False`
#. §8: `contradiction`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L07_Contradiction.lean:34
msgid ""
"Explain that §0 is a statement and §1 is the assumption that §2 is true.\n"
"Remind that §3 is always false. Explain that assumption §4 is false. As any statement\n"
"§5 can be proven from a false assumption. Point out contradiction between §6 and §7\n"
"by applying §8."
msgstr ""
"**You**: If I'm reading this correctly, §0 is a statement, and we also have an assumption called §1, which says … \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: … which says that §2 is true. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: I thought §3 is never true. \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**:  Exactly. The assumption §4 is plainly false. And as you know, you can prove anything starting from a false assumption! In particular, you can prove the statement we are looking for, §5. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: And how do I explain this to our formalosopher? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: I think you need to point out to him that there is a contradiction between the generally accepted belief §6 and his assumption §7. Try §8."

#. §0: `contradiction`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L07_Contradiction.lean:51
msgid "Conclusion Logo L07: There is §0 in assumptions"
msgstr ""
"The first troublemaker seems satisfied. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: Was that a proof by contradiction? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: No, no, not at all.  A proof by contradiction looks different. The argument here was: we have a §0 in our assumptions, from which any statement follows."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L08_Contradiction.lean:15
msgid "Intro Logo L08"
msgstr "Enter second troublemaker."

#. §0: `«{n}» ≠ «{n}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L08_Contradiction.lean:25
msgid "Check that §0 is contradiction by the Contradiction tactic"
msgstr ""
"**You** Isn't §0 also a contradiction? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Perhaps …?"

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L08_Contradiction.lean:42
msgid "Conclusion Logo L08"
msgstr ""
"**You**: Yes, this seems to have worked.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: But somehow this still seems a little suspicious to me. Haven't I just proven that every natural number is equal to 37? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: No, of course not. Only every natural number that is not equal to itself is equal to 37. It's also equal to 38, and to 39, … \n"
"\n"
"**You**: Okay, okay, I understand."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L09_Contradiction.lean:15
msgid "Intro Logo L09"
msgstr "Enter the third troublemaker."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L09_Contradiction.lean:25
msgid "Try the Contradiction tactic"
msgstr ""
"**You**: Another contradiction in the assumptions?\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: You're getting the hang of it."

#. §0: `n ≠ 10`
#. §1: `n = 10`
#. §2: `≠`
#. §3: `¬(· = ·)`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L09_Contradiction.lean:36
msgid "Conclusion Logo L09: §0 is negation of §1. §2 has to be read as §3"
msgstr "**Scribble**: Well done! I guess in this question, the contradiction is a little more obvious: assumption §0 is precisely the negation of §1. You always need to read §2 as §3."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L10_And.lean:15
msgid "Intro Logo L10"
msgstr "The next formalosopher in the queue has brought a question with him. He presents it to us, sits down and starts knitting."

#. §0: `«{A}»`
#. §1: `«{B}»`
#. §2: `trivial`
#. §3: `constructor`
#. §4: `destructor`
#. §5: `constructor`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L10_And.lean:33
msgid ""
"Given assumptions that §0 and §1 are true, prove that 'A and B' is true.\n"
"You cannot just try §2. Use §3 to divide goal into two. Remind that the tactic\n"
"is not called §4. Try §5"
msgstr ""
"**You**: So, we have two assumptions: §0 is true, and §1 is also true. And they want us to prove “§0 and §1”. These formalosophanatics are driving me crazy.  \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**:  You simply need to break the goal into two parts. Try §3.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: You mean §4, perhaps? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: No, §5. I know it's confusing, but that's how they say it."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L10_And.lean:44
msgid "Now you have two goals."
msgstr "**Scribble**: Look, this is magic paper. Now we suddenly have two goals. And I think you already know how to achieve each of them. The goals are listed under *assumptions*."

#. §0: `«{B}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L10_And.lean:46
msgid "Now there is goal §0. This goal is part of the assumption"
msgstr "**Scribble**: Assumption: §0. Goal: §0. Remember `assumption`?"

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L10_And.lean:60
msgid "Conclusion Logo L10"
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: Great! \n"
"\n"
"He seems to be enjoying these questions now. \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Do you think this lever marked “Editor mode” is real? Or is it just a mock-up? Can you try?"

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L11_And.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L11"
msgstr "Slowly, the queue is getting shorter. The next formalosopher, who is also knitting, has the following concern."

#. §0: `obtain ⟨h₁, h₂⟩ := «{h}»`
#. §1: `\\<`
#. §2: `\\>`
#. §3: `\\<>`
#. §4: `h\\1`
#. §5: `h₁`
#. §6: `h₂`
#. §7: `obtain ⟨hA, hBC⟩ := «{h}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L11_And.lean:29
msgid ""
"Deconstruct assumption by §0. The brackets can be written seperatly\n"
"as §1 and §2 or simultaneously as §3. The first assumption can be named §4. Other names\n"
"than §5 und §6 can be chosen as well e.g. §7"
msgstr ""
"**You**: Now we probably have to de-construct the assumption.\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Yes, exactly. The easiest way is with §0.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: Wait, how do I write *that* down here?\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: You write the angle brackets as §1 and §2, or together as §3. And §5 you can write as §4. Or you can simply choose different names for §5 and §6, for example §7."

#. §0: `obtain`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L11_And.lean:35
msgid "[Logo.L11] Hint 2: try §0 tactic"
msgstr "**Scribble**: That already looks better!  And once more!"

#. §0: `assumption`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L11_And.lean:38
msgid "[Logo.L11] Hint 3: try §0 tactic"
msgstr "**Scribble**: You’ve got a proof of that in your §0s."

#. §0: `obtain ⟨h₁, h₂ , h₃⟩ := h`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L11_And.lean:48
msgid "Conclusion Logo L11: Could have been written as §0"
msgstr "**Scribble**: By the way, you could have written that directly as §0."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L12_Or.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L12"
msgstr "Next, please …"

#. §0: `«{A}»`
#. §1: `left`
#. §2: `right`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L12_Or.lean:29
msgid "Try to proof §0 by using §1 | §2"
msgstr ""
"**You**: Do I have to de-construct the proof target again?\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: No, it's much simpler. In order to prove an “or” statement, you just have to decide whether you want to prove the left or right side.\n"
"\n"
"**You**: And how do I explain to my formalosopher which side I would like to prove? Obviously, I want to prove §0! \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: With §1 or §2. Seem's logical enough, doesn't it?"

#. §0: `right`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L12_Or.lean:33
msgid "Try §0 tactic"
msgstr "**Scribble**: Try the other left …"

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L12_Or.lean:43
msgid "Conclusion Logo L12"
msgstr "This formalosopher also leaves happily."

#: Game/Levels/Logo/L13_Or.lean:14
msgid "Intro Logo L13"
msgstr "Next, please …"

#. §0: `(«{A}» ∧ «{B}») ∨ «{A}»`
#. §1: `obtain …`
#. §2: `obtain ⟨h₁, h₂⟩ := «{h}»`
#. §3: `obtain h | h := «{h}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L13_Or.lean:35
msgid ""
"Observe goal as §0.\n"
"Do not try §1 as §2 but as §3"
msgstr ""
"**Scribble**: Look, when you touch an assumption with your finger, it highlights how the brackets are set. Crazy …\n"
"\n"
"**You**: Ah, I see: §0! \n"
"\n"
"**You**: I'm beginning to have my suspicions regarding this whole circus: first an “and” in the target, then “and” in the assumption, then “or” in the target and now “or” in the assumption. They must have agreed on this in advance!\n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: So what? Let’s not spoil their fun. We'll be done here in a moment, and then we can fly on to a more interesting planet. \n"
"\n"
"**You**: So, §1 again? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Yes, but this time §3 rather than §2."

#. §0: `«{A}» ∧ «{B}»`
#. §1: `«{A}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L13_Or.lean:46
msgid "Prove goal once for §0 and once for §1"
msgstr "**Scribble**: Now you have to prove your goal twice: once assuming the left side §0, and once assuming the right side §1. First, we assume that the left side §0 is true."

#. §0: `obtain ⟨h₁, h₂⟩ := «{h}»`
#. §1: `\\<>`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L13_Or.lean:52
msgid "Try §0. Write brackets as §1"
msgstr "**Scribble**: You already know how to handle an 'and' in the assumptions: §0. Remember you can write the angle brackets as §1."

#. §0: `(«{A}» ∧ «{B}») ∨ «{A}»`
#. §1: `«{A}»`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L13_Or.lean:59
msgid "Show goal via §0 i.e. assume §1 is true"
msgstr "**Scribble**: Now you have to prove the goal assuming the right half of §0, i.e. assuming that §1 is true."

#. §0: `ℕ`
#. §1: `0, 1, 2, …`
#. §2: `=`
#. §3: `≠`
#. §4: `¬(·=·)`
#. §5: `Prop`
#. §6: `True`
#. §7: `(True : Prop)`
#. §8: `False`
#. §9: `(False : Prop)`
#. §10: `¬`
#. §11: `∧`
#. §12: `∨`
#. §13: `(n : ℕ)`
#. §14: `(A : Prop)`
#. §15: `(ha : A)`
#. §16: `(A : Prop)`
#. §17: `(h : A ∧ B)`
#. §18: `h`
#. §19: `rfl`
#. §20: `A = A`
#. §21: `assumption`
#. §22: `contradiction`
#. §23: `decide`
#. §24: `constructor`
#. §25: `left`
#. §26: `right`
#. §27: `obtain ⟨h₁, h₂⟩ := h`
#. §28: `obtain h := h \\| h`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L13_Or.lean:116
msgid ""
"Conclusion Logo L13:\n"
"Notations introdced so far:\n"
"§0, §1, §2, §3 (shorthand for §4), §5, §6 (§7 is always true),\n"
"§8 (§9 is always false), §10, §11, §12, §13, §14, §15 (Proof that §16 is true),\n"
" §17 (Assumption with name §18).\n"
"Tactics introduced so far:\n"
"§19 (proves §20), §21, §22, §23, §24, §25/§26, §27, §28"
msgstr ""
"**You**: Okay, that seems to have satisfied him. Just one more soul … \n"
"\n"
"**You**:  Before we go, could you briefly summarise everything you've taught me so far? \n"
"\n"
"Scribble is beaming with joy. You've *never* been so dependent on him. \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Sure! Here goes.\n"
"\n"
"## Terminology & notation\n"
"\n"
"|                   | description | \n"
"|:--------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------| \n"
"| *goal* | whatever currently needs to be proven | \n"
"| §0 | the type of all natural numbers | \n"
"| §1 | some explicit natural numbers | \n"
"| §13 | an arbitrary natural number | \n"
"| §2 | equality | \n"
"| §3 | inequality: abbreviation for §4 |\n"
"| §5 | the type of all logical statements | \n"
"| §6 | the statement that is always true | \n"
"| §8 | the statement that is always false | \n"
"| §14 | an arbitrary logical statement | \n"
"| §10 | logical negation | \n"
"| §11 | logical AND |\n"
"| §12 | logical OR | \n"
"| §15 | proof that the logical statement §16 is true | \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"## Tactics \n"
"\n"
"The words you need to actively use are collectively called tactics. Here are all the tactics we have used on this planet: \n"
"\n"
"| tactic | example | \n"
"|:--------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------| \n"
"| §19 | proves §20 | \n"
"| §21 | looks for the goal among the assumptions |\n"
"| §22 | looks for a contradiction. | \n"
"| §23 | tries to decide algorithmically whether a statement is true | \n"
"| §24 | splits an AND in the goal | \n"
"| §25/§26 | initiates a proof of one side of an OR in the goal |\n"
"| §27 | splits an AND in an assumption | \n"
"| §28 | splits an OR in an assumption into two cases | \n"
"\n"
"**You**: How do you know all of this? \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: No idea. I think it was pre-installed."

#. §0: `∧`
#. §1: `∨`
#. §2: `obtain ⟨h₁, h₂⟩ := h`
#. §3: `obtain h \\| h := h`
#. §4: `constructor`
#. §5: `left`
#. §6: `right`
#: Game/Levels/Logo/L14_Summary.lean:24
msgid ""
"Intro Logo L14: The most important tactics of this chapter concerning §0 and §1.\n"
"For both logical operations the tactis §2 and §3 can be used respectivelly.\n"
"Analogously, when working with the goal §4 and §5/§6 can be used respectivelly as well."
msgstr ""
"The last subject steps forward. Her request is somewhat more complicated than the previous ones. \n"
"\n"
"**Scribble**: Just throw everything at it that you've learned. Here, I'll try to be helpful by showing you the four most important tactics for this situation again. \n"
"\n"
"| (Overview) | And (§0) | Or (§1) | \n"
"|:------------|:-------------------------|:------------------------| \n"
"| Assumption | §2 | §3 | \n"
"| Goal | §4 | §5/§6 | "

